# Single-Cycle Processor in Verilog

A single-cycle processor implemented in Verilog HDL, inspired by the MIPS architecture. This project was developed as part of a Computer Architecture course.

## Overview

This processor executes each instruction in a single clock cycle, meaning that fetch, decode, execute, memory access, and write-back all happen within one clock period. While simpler than pipelined designs, it provides a clear and educational foundation for understanding how a CPU works at the hardware level.

The architecture follows classic MIPS design principles, including:

- Program Counter (PC) for instruction addressing
- Instruction Memory for fetching instructions
- Register File with general-purpose registers
- ALU for arithmetic and logic operations
- Data Memory for load/store operations
- Control Unit for generating control signals based on the opcode

## Project Structure

### Core Modules
| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `Datapath.v` | Top-level datapath connecting all components |
| `PC_Unit.v` | Program Counter unit |
| `Subleq_ALU_Pure_Gates.v` | ALU implemented using pure logic gates |
| `register16.v` / `register48.v` | Register modules with different bit widths |
| `data_memory.v` | Data memory for load/store operations |
| `instruction_memory.v` | Instruction memory for fetching instructions |

### Building Blocks
| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `Fulladder.v` / `Fulladder16.v` | Full adder circuits |
| `subtractor.v` | Subtractor circuit |
| `d_flipflop.v` | D flip-flop |
| `Counter_1bit.v` | 1-bit counter |
| `decoder5to32.v` | 5-to-32 decoder |
| `split48_to_3x16.v` | Splits a 48-bit signal into three 16-bit parts |
| `mux2to1.v` / `mux2to1_16bit.v` / `mux2to1_48bit.v` | 2-to-1 multiplexers |
| `mux32to1_16bit.v` / `mux32to1_48bit.v` | 32-to-1 multiplexers |

### Testbenches
| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `tb_data_memory.v` | Testbench for data memory |
| `tb_instruction_memory.v` | Testbench for instruction memory |
| `test_pc_fixed.v` | Testbench for the PC unit |
| `test_splitter.v` | Testbench for the splitter module |
| `test_subtractor.v` | Testbench for the subtractor |

## Features

- Single-cycle datapath and control
- MIPS-inspired instruction set
- Modular Verilog design with separate modules for each component
- Synthesizable RTL code
- Testbenches provided for simulation and verification

## Purpose

This project was developed for a Computer Architecture course to demonstrate fundamental concepts of processor design, including:

- Datapath and control design
- Instruction execution flow
- RTL design using Verilog
- Simulation and verification of hardware designs

## Tools Used

- **Quartus** – for writing and developing the Verilog code
- **ModelSim** – for simulating and verifying the design
- **Verilog HDL** – for hardware description

